<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truth and Reconciliation Commission (Peru)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2001">2001</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was established in 2001, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a>, to investigate the internal armed conflict of 1980–2000. Its final report of 2003 estimated that about 69,000 people had been killed or disappeared, attributed more than half of the deaths to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> and many to the security forces, and found that three-quarters of the victims spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> or another native language. It recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and prosecutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru.jpg" alt="Truth and Reconciliation Commission (Peru)" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johnattan Rupire · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Placa_conmemorativa_de_la_Comisi%C3%B3n_de_la_Verdad_y_Reconciliaci%C3%B3n_Nacional_en_Ayacucho.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
